  "worked_solution": "All of these vector-valued functions are defined at $t=0.$ \n\nA vector-valued function is differentiable at a point if and only if each of its components is differentiable at that point. \n\nLet's consider the functions one-by-one. \n\n- We differentiate $f(t)$ to find ${f}^{\u2032}(0).$\nNote, that the derivative of $|t-1|$ at  $t=0$ coincides with the derivative of $(1-t),$ so, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(0) & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(7t+6),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3)\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8-1,\\,7,\\,0\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince each component of ${f}^{\u2032}(t)$ is  defined  at $t=0$, we conclude that $f(t)$ is differentiable at this point.\n\n- Next, we differentiate $g(t)$  to find ${g}^{\u2032}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{\u2032}(t) & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e80,\\,2t,\\,1\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince each component of ${g}^{\u2032}(t)$ is defined   at $t=0$, we conclude that $g(t)$ is differentiable at this point.\n\n- Finally, we differentiate $h(t)$ to find ${h}^{\u2032}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{\u2032}(t) & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}+1),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt[3]{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t+3)\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e83{t}^{2},\\,\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{t}^{2}}},\\,2\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second component of this vector, $\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{t}^{2}}}$, is undefined at $t=0$,  we conclude that the function $h(t)$ is not differentiable at $t=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$f(t)$ and $g(t)$ only.\"",
